A||N||8||Countach||Anyone install 4.5" bolt pattern wheels?||||1||
Z||000000||Countach||11-13-2001||01:26 AM||||I found some wheels for my kitcar, but they only come in 4.5, instead of Fiero's stock 100mm.  One store I won't mention their name (Discount Tire) said it was exactly the same.  Another (Big O) said they'd sell them, but not warrenty them.  They said they just had a kid come in with his Grand Am (100mm) and have to have the wheels cut off 'cause the lugs were frozen.  I was thinking I could drill the holes out SLIGHTLY, and be okay.  The only thing that would bother me a bit would be knowing the shoulder of the bolt holes is a bit out of wack.  These Eagle Alloy cast aluminim wheels will save me $2000, so I'm rather motivated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||watts||11-13-2001||02:28 AM||||Well, 4.5" is 114.3mm - that's about 9/16th's of an inch out!  Not a small amount to be "exactly the same".  Stupid mofo's (un-named tire place).<P>If these are a real steal of a deal on the mags, inquire at some shops that could re-drill them to the right diameter of bolt circle.  Make sure the hub is the right diameter too - harder to correct that.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||11-13-2001||10:25 AM||||it's possible and supposivly easy in the rear, more work and cash in front. i've been asking myself but haven't started hunting up the parts yet. do a search in this section or the archives of this section with 6000 swap or 6000 brakes as the search words. for names put in mine ('cause i've asked a few times) or jay or rockcrawl or leave blank. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||3fifty||11-13-2001||11:13 PM||||Go to a search engine and search for Salesco.<BR>They will build composite wheels to order that will fit Fiero kit cars cheap.||||reg||10
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||11-14-2001||11:24 AM||||spend about $200. dollors and get wheel adapters for all four wheels||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mike Murphy||11-14-2001||07:31 PM||||Wheel adapters are not a bad idea if you do not mind the tire sticking out past the body several inches if you are using a stock body.<P>I've been trying to figure out the best way to put a set of 16 inch wheels off of an 86 Trans Am and I'm definitly not using the wheel adapter as I would have 3 to 4 inches excess.<P>Different hubs and rotors with the proper spacing of the studs would be best.  I've seen some total conversions that would cost over $2500 but doesn't seem like best use of my money.<P>Any one else got an inexpensive fix for this problem?<BR>Mike Murphy||||reg||11
Z||000006||cadaver||11-14-2001||09:47 PM||||NEVER USE WHEEL ADAPTERS. I have seen even well made expensive ones fail.<BR>Either redrill the rims. Not enlarge the holes bit have them done right.<BR>Or redrill or swap the hubs and rotors <BR>Or get different rims.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JB||11-14-2001||10:21 PM||||I agree with cadaver.  I would have a machine shop redrill the rotors.||||reg||1
Z||000008||RedOktober||11-17-2001||02:59 AM||||I would suggest that you look for a better wheel package, Compomotive makes great replica wheels for your Countach. They even have up to 15x12 wheels for the car that look very original. Remember that you have almost 12" of width to fill up.<BR>Ive used the 100 mm to 4 3/4 wheel adaptors(dodge mags)on a few cars and they have never given me any problems for speeds up to 120mph.But dont expect to be doing that all day long!!!<P>Also, from experience, the Fiero does not like too wide of a front tire. The bump steer cannot handle the extra feed back theat you get from potholes and bumps. <BR>Good luck.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by RedOktober (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
